  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「ギュウシンリ」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%82%AE%E3%83%A5%E3%82%A6%E3%82%B7%E3%83%B3%E3%83%AA",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 109234082,
      "text": "ギュウシンリ は、バンレイシ科バンレイシ属の植物の1種である。中南米原産とされるが、果実が食用とされ、世界中の熱帯域で栽培されている。ギュウシンリ（牛心梨）や英名の bullock's heart、ox-heart の名は、熟した果実が赤褐色で心臓形であることに由来する（図1）。カスタードアップル とも呼ばれるが、チェリモヤやバンレイシなどバンレイシ属の別種もカスタードアップルと呼ばれることがある。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Annona reticulata」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Annona_reticulata",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1375787806,
      "text": "Annona reticulata is a small deciduous or semi-evergreen tree in the plant family Annonaceae. It is best known for its fruit, called custard apple, a common name shared with fruits of several other species in the same genus: A. cherimola and A. squamosa. Other English common names include ox heart and bullock's heart. The fruit is sweet and useful in preparation of desserts, but is generally less popular for eating than that of A. cherimola."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Annona reticulata」の節「Cultivation」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Annona_reticulata#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1375787806,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "Seeds can be propagated. Other techniques for cultivation are grafting and budding. The tree produces 45 kg of fruits per year. In Asia, the season lasts from July to September, and in the Caribbean, it runs from February to April. Annona reticulata can take 2-3 years from planting time to harvest.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
  },
